INT. SUDS AND SPIN LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Midnight. The street outside is empty and dark. Inside, one fluorescent light buzzes above a row of dryers, all of them still. A single machine in the back tumbles slowly, the clothes inside going around and around.

Tom, 20s, sits on a plastic chair with a gym bag between his feet, a flashlight and a half-eaten granola bar in his lap. He has not spoken in a long time.

On the other side of the room, a figure in a wet raincoat sits with her back to the wall, a hood pulled low. THE STRANGER has been there since the power went out two days ago. Neither of them has said why they are here.

TOM
(finally)
Is that machine still working?

THE STRANGER
Just the one. I think it's on a different circuit.

TOM
Huh.

A long pause. The dryer thumps. Water drips somewhere behind the wall.

TOM (CONT'D)
How much food do you have left?

THE STRANGER
Not a lot. Some crackers. A can of beans I can't open.

TOM
I have a can opener. On my keys.

THE STRANGER
(a short breath that is almost a laugh)
Of course you do.

Tom stands, crosses the room, and holds out the keyring. She takes it without looking up. The can opens with a tiny, rusty squeak.

THE STRANGER (CONT'D)
(pushing back her hood)
Thanks. I'm Dana, by the way.

TOM
Tom.

DANA
I know. It's on your bag.

TOM
(glancing down, almost smiling)
Right.

They sit in silence for a while, the only sounds the dryer and the dripping water. Dana eats a spoonful of beans, offers the can. Tom takes it, eats, hands it back.

DANA
You think anyone's coming?

TOM
I don't know. Eventually.

DANA
Eventually is a long time.

TOM
(quietly)
It's all we've got.

The dryer finally slows. It clunks, groans, and stops. The room is utterly still. Dana looks at the dead machine, then at Tom.

DANA
Well. At least the towels are warm.

Tom laughs, a real one, small and cracked. Dana laughs too. Outside, far off, a single siren starts up and fades away again.
